      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "text": "On 21 March 2025, Dubai Executive Council Resolution No. 11, \"Regulating the Conduct of Free Zone Establishments - Activities within the Emirate of Dubai,\" was officially published and entered into force (\"Resolution\"). It expands opportunities for companies registered in Dubai’s free zones to conduct business outside the free zone. Pursuant to the Resolution, companies from all of Dubai’s free zones will be able to apply to the Dubai Department of Economy and Tourism for a licence or permit to conduct business in the mainland of Dubai. \n\nThe Resolution provides for the option to obtain two types of licences to open a branch of a free zone company, and a temporary permit to carry out certain types of activities in the mainland of Dubai without opening a branch. A list of permitted activities under such licences and permits is to be published within the next six months. \n\nAs a rule, those companies that are residents of UAE free zones are permitted to conduct business only within the free zone where they are registered, and outside the UAE. \n\nAt the same time, some of Dubai’s free zones allow for a dual-licensing model, under which companies that are residents of the free zones can obtain a second licence to operate in the mainland of Dubai, in addition to their licence to operate within the free zone. However, the availability of this option is materially limited: of the more than 20 Dubai free zones, only a few allow companies to hold two licences. \n\nThe new regulations are expected to be a significant step toward simplifying the process for companies to transition from free zones to Dubai’s domestic market. \n\nPlease note that this Resolution applies only within Dubai and does not cover other emirates of the UAE. Financial institutions registered with the Dubai International Financial Centre (DIFC) are also excluded from the scope of the Resolution.",
